1
00:00:01,480 --> 00:00:02,900
It's 8.30, guys.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:05,140
The 8 o 'clock bus will be here in 10 minutes.

3
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:06,640
I still gotta get ready.

4
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:10,460
Ben, school. Forgot to give you guys this.

5
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:12,120
It's a letter from the Parents Association.

6
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:13,640
Ixnay on the Etterly, Carol.

7
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:16,180
I'll take it.

8
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:17,180
Let me see that.

9
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:22,240
Dear fellow parents, our program of weekly dances is in jeopardy, owing to the fact

10
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:25,940
that many of you have not fulfilled your parental responsibilities and made

11
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:28,040
yourselves available for chaperoning.

12
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:32,900
You know who you are, and more importantly, we know who you are.

13
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:36,040
Sincerely, Mrs. Adolf Hitler.

14
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:40,880
Oh! June Hinkley, president of the Parent Association.

15
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:42,700
Nice tone. Now, come on.

16
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:46,960
Now, busy people like you guys have far better things to do than to hang around the

17
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:47,960
smelly gym and...

18
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,900
Embarrassing, Mike. Yeah!

19
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:51,040
I mean, no.

20
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:54,860
Embarrass? That was her word, not my word, Mom.

21
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,800
I don't care if they chaperone dances.

22
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:01,380
Bye. It's because you've never been to one.

23
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:04,900
Mike, did you get one of these letters?

24
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:08,560
Yeah, sure. Well, where is it?

25
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:12,160
Come on, Mom, give me some kind of credit here.

26
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:15,100
Look, I'm not going to bother you guys with a stupid letter like that.

27
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:17,560
Look, I try to screen these things for you guys.

28
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:20,480
You know, cut down on the junk mail.

29
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:22,040
Protect your valuable time.

30
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:25,740
It's stuck to the ceiling of the boy's jar.

31
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:31,160
Show me that smile again.

32
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,620
Don't waste another minute on your crying.

33
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,880
We're nowhere near the end.

34
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:43,080
The fest is ready to begin.

35
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:53,200
Oh. As long as we got each other, we got the world spinning light in our hands.

36
00:01:53,340 --> 00:02:00,400
Baby, you and me, we gotta be the last couple The luckiest dreamers who never quit

37
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:08,900
dreaming. As long as we keep on giving, we can take anything that comes our way.

38
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:19,280
Baby, rain or shine, all the time, we got each other sharing the laughter and love.

39
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:30,820
Ben, the bus.

40
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:32,120
Use it or lose it.

41
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:36,680
Come on, Ben, I think you're spending far too much time studying and not nearly

42
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:37,800
enough horsing around.

43
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:39,640
Hey. I'm kidding.

44
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:41,360
Ben knows when the old dad is kidding.

45
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:43,400
Right, Ben? I'm a lord.

46
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:44,920
I'm a lord?

47
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:46,140
It's from Robin Hood.

48
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:47,840
That's the school play this year.

49
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:51,860
Oh, you mean you weren't calling me a lord as a measure of respect?

50
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:53,520
Ha ha ha ha.

51
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:56,460
That's funny, Dad.

52
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:00,120
See, he does know when his old dad's kidding.

53
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,320
This year, I'm gonna get a part.

54
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:06,960
Well, let's hope, but I remember last year's auditions, how heartbroken you were.

55
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:08,840
Come on, Dad, I was just a kid.

56
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:10,340
Ah, I see.

57
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:12,520
So this year is the only adult in the third grade.

58
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:13,480
You ought to have a big edge.

59
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:15,080
That's how I figure it.

60
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:15,880
Well, good luck.

61
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:20,500
Bye, honey. Break a leg.

62
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:26,140
What? Figure of speech.

63
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:33,920
It's... Jason, when you were Mike's age, would you have been afraid or embarrassed

64
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:35,260
to have your parents chaperone?

65
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:37,240
Oh, sure. I went through that stage.

66
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:40,180
My dad volunteered to chaperone one of our class trips.

67
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:41,500
I begged him not to.

68
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:43,680
He said, Dad, but I'm not gonna do anything.

69
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:44,580
He said, well, that's fine.

70
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:46,260
Then you can not do it with me there.

71
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:49,760
Hey, looking good, Dad.

72
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:51,420
Vicious ensemble, Mom.

73
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:54,040
It was a nice day and night here.

74
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:58,440
Halt. Mom, I might miss that darn bus.

75
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:00,580
Mm -hmm. Open the coat.

76
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:10,300
Is this cool or what?

77
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:14,240
Mike, do you really think you've dressed appropriately for school?

78
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:16,540
Heck, no. Why would I want to do that?

79
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:19,900
Change now. Dad.

80
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:21,620
Mike, I like it.

81
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:25,720
If you think that this conveys the proper image, not just for yourself, but for the

82
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:30,440
family you represent, then by all means, go ahead and wear a rubber foul on your

83
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:33,000
stomach. See ya.

84
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:39,820
Jason. I don't know why that works, but it's really starting to burn me up.

85
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:48,420
You know, um, you shouldn't judge the school by the first day.

86
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:51,900
And you have to overlook all the average people.

87
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:54,460
It is a public school.

88
00:04:56,120 --> 00:04:57,740
Well, I'm used to that, Carol.

89
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:01,760
You'd be surprised how many average people there are in Los Angeles.

90
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:08,320
My old science teacher wouldn't even let me build a small model of a thermonuclear

91
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:09,720
device for a science project.

92
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:12,320
Vision or fusion?

93
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:13,600
Oh, fusion, of course.

94
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:17,360
Of course. I plan to fit in at this school.

95
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:19,940
Sure. I've already joined the dance decoration committee.

96
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:26,620
Oh? Will you be, um, at the dance then tomorrow night?

97
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:31,240
Sure. Will you, um, be dancing?

98
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:35,180
I haven't made up my mind yet.

99
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:36,820
I might just observe.

100
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:41,860
Hi, Mike. Peace, Gunn -Greth.

101
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:53,940
Brother. Mike, is that you?

102
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:55,820
What'd I do?

103
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:01,840
Nothing. What do you think?

104
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:03,280
It's you, Dad.

105
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:06,100
Yeah. That's what all my patients said.

106
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:11,340
Dad, Dad, wait till you...

107
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:13,620
Up here, Ben.

108
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:18,040
I'm sorry. Dad, I got a part!

109
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:19,060
I got cast!

110
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:20,300
Hey, all right.

111
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:21,060
Good for you.

112
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:22,720
What, did you get one of Robin's merry men?

113
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,040
Nope. Friar Tuck.

114
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:26,460
Nope. Better. Not Robin?

115
00:06:26,540 --> 00:06:29,040
No, better. What?

116
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:33,820
A rock. A rock?

117
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:35,660
An English rock.

118
00:06:37,420 --> 00:06:41,120
Well, I hope the hours required rehearsing this part won't interfere with your

119
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:43,640
studies. That's the best part.

120
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:49,140
I can be in the play, go to all the parties, and all I have to do is lay there.

121
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:51,860
My son, the rock.

122
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:57,560
Yeah! Now, Jason, I want to talk to you about this shirt.

123
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:01,460
Now, if this is the proper image you wish to convey, not only for yourself, but for

124
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:02,700
the family you represent.

125
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:03,940
Okay, all right.

126
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:06,220
All right. It's a shame, though.

127
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:10,640
I was thinking of wearing this when your mom and I chaperoned your school dance.

128
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:14,680
What? You're not really gonna chaperone.

129
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:16,860
Had you going there for a second, didn't I?

130
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:20,500
Bill! Put her there.

131
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:24,500
All right. Hey, wait a minute.

132
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:26,300
What's this? What?

133
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:29,800
For the first time in your lives, you two agree on something.

134
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:35,120
That's better. I thought you said you didn't care whether they chaperoned or not.

135
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:36,780
Oh, I don't.

136
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:40,340
Really. Hey, Carol, you don't even go to these dances.

137
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:42,720
Well, I might go to this one.

138
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:44,920
I'm keeping my options open.

139
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:48,480
And she just wants to go because that geek out on the front porch wants to go.

140
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:50,520
He's not a geek.

141
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:51,820
He's from Los Angeles.

142
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:56,440
Oh. You're breathing heavy for him, Carol.

143
00:07:58,160 --> 00:07:59,160
I am not.

144
00:07:59,220 --> 00:08:02,260
We were discussing nuclear weapons.

145
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:05,080
See? Perfectly harmless.

146
00:08:06,700 --> 00:08:10,660
You're just afraid that if Mom and Dad chaperone, You won't be able to wear makeup

147
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:13,940
and go slow dancing and run off into the parking lot with that goon.

148
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:16,160
Michael! Parking lot?

149
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:18,320
Night of the girl?

150
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:21,620
Heavy breathing? Los Angeles?

151
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:23,780
Don't listen to him, Dad.

152
00:08:24,060 --> 00:08:26,320
You know, chaperoning is not all bad.

153
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:30,480
What? Dad, I'm not gonna do anything.

154
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:33,700
Fine. Then you can not do it with me there.

155
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:38,480
If you want to hit me, I won't stop you.

156
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:49,320
See, I knew it.

157
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:50,180
I can't wear this.

158
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:52,680
Chaperones are supposed to look stodgy and respectable.

159
00:08:53,220 --> 00:08:54,500
Well, then why don't you wear this one?

160
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:57,840
You think that dress is stodgy?

161
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:02,200
This one? Stodgy?

162
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:03,500
Ha! I'm kidding.

163
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:04,740
I love this dress.

164
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:06,000
It's my favorite dress.

165
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:07,640
I have dreams about this dress.

166
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:14,340
Okay. Oh, sweetheart, I haven't had the chance to tell you how surprised and happy I

167
00:09:14,340 --> 00:09:17,060
was that you changed your mind and agreed to do this.

168
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:18,900
It shows real maturity.

169
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:22,920
Well, it's me, mature yet curiously juvenile.

170
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:28,580
And, of course, the fact that Carol's going had nothing to do with it.

171
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:31,280
Carol who? It's okay.

172
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:36,320
It's okay. I love you when you're hopelessly old -fashioned.

173
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:39,180
Mm -hmm. Mm.

174
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:44,700
Just... You realize this is exactly the sort of behavior we're supposed to prevent

175
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:47,740
tomorrow night? Mm -hmm.

176
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:48,540
But this is tonight?

177
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:50,360
Mm -hmm. Mm -hmm.

178
00:09:51,780 --> 00:09:53,360
Isn't life crazy?

179
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:56,740
I mean, I meet you for the first time yesterday, and then I find out that we're on

180
00:09:56,740 --> 00:09:58,060
the same decorating committee.

181
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:00,940
Crazy, crazy, crazy!

182
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:05,760
I'm sorry. My parents are chaperoning tonight.

183
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:10,880
I'm sorry. Thank you.

184
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,180
It came as quite a shock.

185
00:10:12,420 --> 00:10:15,420
I can imagine my parents chaperoned once.

186
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:17,000
How was it?

187
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:18,360
It was mortifying.

188
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:23,560
They...Carol, they danced.

189
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:28,280
Oh, my God.

190
00:10:29,580 --> 00:10:34,000
My father started flailing his arms like some huge thrashing machine.

191
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:39,180
My mother, she kind of hopped and clapped and yelled, hey, in time to the music.

192
00:10:41,860 --> 00:10:43,680
What a nightmare.

193
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:51,640
Ward? Mrs. Hinkley.

194
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:54,780
You've done our school proud, as usual.

195
00:10:55,240 --> 00:10:56,580
Thank you, Ward.

196
00:10:56,580 --> 00:11:00,880
If all the ladies in the Parents Association were like you, it would sure change my

197
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:04,080
life. Thank you.

198
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:06,500
Uh, we loved your chaperone letter.

199
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:07,780
It really did the trick.

200
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:10,580
Wonderful. Who is chaperoning tonight?

201
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:12,960
The Seavers. Ward.

202
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:16,060
Uh, yes? I don't know.

203
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:21,620
Maybe it's okay for a man to run a psychiatric practice out of the home, and maybe

204
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:26,040
it's all right for a woman to go back to work just when her children need her most.

205
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:30,720
and maybe letting our offspring run wild is hunky -dory.

206
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:33,880
And maybe I'm just old -fashioned.

207
00:11:34,580 --> 00:11:36,880
Could be. Ward.

208
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:40,460
Ward, I'm worried about the Sievers.

209
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:48,400
I mean, the type of people we need for Chaperone should be able to control their own

210
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:51,860
children. Wow, that's a pretty high standard.

211
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:55,800
Boy, your parents sound worse than mine.

212
00:11:57,480 --> 00:12:00,320
Oh, shit. I'm sorry.

213
00:12:06,860 --> 00:12:07,860
I'm sorry. Hi, honey.

214
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:10,060
Hi. You're home early.

215
00:12:10,700 --> 00:12:11,640
Slow news day.

216
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:15,900
Oh, well, then the lord and lady of the manor have the castle all to themselves.

217
00:12:15,940 --> 00:12:18,740
Mm -hmm. Hey, guys!

218
00:12:18,740 --> 00:12:19,300
How you doing?

219
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:21,320
I'll get it.

220
00:12:21,320 --> 00:12:22,720
That does it.

221
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:24,220
I quit the play.

222
00:12:25,080 --> 00:12:28,040
Aw, Ben, you're not going to be a rock after all.

223
00:12:28,380 --> 00:12:32,960
No way. Friar Tuck sits on me all through Act Two.

224
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:37,020
Well, uh, that's okay.

225
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:38,740
Then we can chaperone another time.

226
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:41,580
Mm -hmm. Well, uh, thank you for calling, Mrs.

227
00:12:41,620 --> 00:12:45,000
Hitler. Hankley. All right.

228
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:48,200
Bye. You mean we aren't chaperoning?

229
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:50,540
No. Oh, somebody pinched me.

230
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:52,000
I'm dreaming. This is great.

231
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:55,180
No, it's not.

232
00:12:55,940 --> 00:12:57,040
What are you, nuts?

233
00:12:57,460 --> 00:12:58,820
I forget I already know the answer.

234
00:13:00,020 --> 00:13:01,400
Did she tell you why?

235
00:13:01,780 --> 00:13:04,620
Well, apparently they've double booked the chaperones this week.

236
00:13:05,220 --> 00:13:06,940
Well, she lied to you.

237
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:11,420
The reason you're not chaperoning is because...

238
00:13:11,420 --> 00:13:12,920
you're unacceptable parents.

239
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:17,320
Unacceptable parents? Carol, you heard this?

240
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:18,560
Not just me.

241
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:21,580
Scotty Siegler heard every word of it.

242
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:28,680
All right! I don't get it.

243
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:32,100
She said, I have a mother who abandoned me, a brother who's a delinquent, a father

244
00:13:32,100 --> 00:13:33,440
who runs a mental ward at home.

245
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:37,600
She made my life sound like a movie of the week.

246
00:13:39,740 --> 00:13:40,980
I'll kill her.

247
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:42,560
Maggie, don't get upset.

248
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:45,560
Jason, how can you stand there so calmly?

249
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:49,440
Actually, I have deeply rooted feelings of anger and hostility.

250
00:13:50,460 --> 00:13:53,120
I just refuse to give vent to them until we find out if there's some

251
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:55,620
misunderstanding. I'll give her a vent.

252
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:58,260
So long over school by tomorrow.

253
00:13:58,460 --> 00:13:59,560
Oh, Carol, it will not.

254
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:00,700
How's anybody gonna hear?

255
00:14:00,980 --> 00:14:02,000
No, I mean, I'm not kidding.

256
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:02,760
That's what they said.

257
00:14:03,220 --> 00:14:05,960
